{"name":"Moon Rise","hex":"#f4f4e8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moon rise","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of moonrise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon_rise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rise (zero gravity indicator)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Rise\" is a small stuffed toy used as a weightlessness indicator for the Artemis II lunar flyby mission and as a mascot for its crew. The indicator was created based on a design by 8-year-old Californian Lucas Ye, whose work won a NASA-sponsored competition held from March to June 2025. The toy design is inspired by the photograph Earthrise taken by Apollo 8 astronaut William Anders on December 24, 1968, and is copyrighted by NASA."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Rise_Mascot.jpg/330px-Rise_Mascot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rise_(zero_gravity_indicator)","wikidata":"Q139070507"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg/500px-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg/1280px-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg/500px-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_%28KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jeremy Hansen, a member of the Artemis II crew, holds the talisman on March 27, 2026.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArtemis_II_Crew_Arrives_at_KSC_Ahead_of_Launch_(KSC-04272026-Artemis_II_Crew_Arrival-8).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-20260329-PH-KLS01_0034%29.jpg/500px-Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-20260329-PH-KLS01_0034%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-20260329-PH-KLS01_0034%29.jpg/1280px-Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-20260329-PH-KLS01_0034%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-20260329-PH-KLS01_0034%29.jpg/500px-Artemis_II_Crew_Q_and_A_and_Zero_Gravity_Indicator_%28KSC-